[
  {
    "number": 57,
    "title": "Support a fresh Linux installation of Nanoleaf services",
    "body": "Adds a fresh Linux installation of the existing Nanoleaf services. Hooks, CLI, wall map and controller share private Linux SQLite; the existing on-demand worker remains the sole light writer. The separate Node MCP host uses its existing direct HTTP transport. Linux runtime commands no longer need Windows executable forwarding.\n\nImplements #54. Installed WSL, real-client and physical acceptance remains #55. [Hub PR #84](https://github.com/jimmie-potts/agent-device-hub/pull/84) is the architecture/work-guide companion for Hub #43. Guide synchronization remains pending until that PR merges. This does not import old data, change the wall presentation, add a combined daemon or hook API, or adopt shared monitoring.\n\nThe installer creates a private Python environment, copies the required runtime and selected native Node 24, builds the pinned MCP package, preserves unrelated hooks, accepts configured Desktop JSON paths, and generates three user service units. It starts no services or worker and sends only a device layout read. The operator must retire the Windows owner before activating Linux hooks or services. Defaults are wall 8765, controller 41231 and MCP 41230. Port collisions fail visibly; Linux map commands print their URL.\n\nValidation:\n\n- Focused failing-then-passing tests cover explicit hook/setup state selection, fixed map ports, duplicate owners and stale receipts, private installer state, bounded token input, runtime/hook generation, credential separation, service definitions and MCP port collisions.\n- The complete isolated installer ran with real venv/pip/npm and a loopback fake Nanoleaf device. Separate installed foreground services served the wall map and authenticated controller/MCP requests. Eight worker processes respected an existing exclusive lock. MCP Quiet/Work/Free commands settled and restored the fake scene and brightness.\n- Local checks pass: 173 Python tests at current head `7c06f6555050ffe058be9493a71a7de9f5cc94e0`. The 39 MCP tests with build/type/artifact checks passed at `4abafec`; the subsequent two-file change only corrects the Python installer filesystem guard and its regression test. Browser checks using the documented override and cached Chromium headless shell, workflow checks with seven specifications and ten workflow tests, and static systemd unit verification passed before the focused filesystem/description fixes; those areas are unchanged. Loopback was excluded from the task's HTTP proxy for local checks.\n- All 18 configured push/PR checks passed at the prior head `b1653cf8522f8df65302a0bb7ac11fe7fb4575a1`, including hosted Windows and browser jobs. GitHub refused to start all 18 current-head jobs because of an account payment or spending-limit problem. Current-head CI must pass before merge. No local Windows, ordinary-WSL systemd, actual Codex client or physical-light acceptance is claimed.\n\nThe issue-linked OpenSpec change `gh-54-linux-fresh-install` is synchronized and archived. Independent Standards and Specification reviews have no P0–P3 findings at base/merge-base `cdceffddb7a86c138797325e8d6b0460eb320e58` and head `7c06f6555050ffe058be9493a71a7de9f5cc94e0`. Specification verifies A1–A7. See the [final review receipt](https://github.com/jimmie-potts/codex-nanoleaf/pull/57#issuecomment-5610892283). The installer/ownership ADR is `docs/decisions/0007-linux-runtime-ownership.md`; operator instructions are `docs/linux-install.md`. The current main connector-geometry change is retained.\n",
    "state": "open",
